  "query_preview": "..(Tonight Through Wednesday)\nIssued at 947 PM MDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\n04Z upper level analysis has high amplitude trof across the West \nCoast, with strong closed low over northern California at the \nmoment. Zonal flow currently resides across the Northern Plains. \nSkies are mostly clear across the area, with some thin cirrus \nmoving in from the central Rockies. At the surface, high pressure \nis present across the Dakotas, with a weak frontal boundary just \nto the south of the forecast area. Temperatures at 10pm are in the\n50s and 60s with light north to northeast winds.\n\nQuiet and mild conditions will continue into the morning hours as\nweak ridging holds across the area. Some patchy fog may persist\ninto the early morning hours across the South Dakota plains.\nTemperatures today will mainly be in the 70s to around 80 \ndegrees, with cloud cover increasing through the day as the next \nsystem moves into the area. Strong shortwave in southwest flow \nwill lift into the Northern Plains late today, bringing rain \nshowers and isolated thunderstorms to much of the forecast area. A\nfew stronger storms are possible, but widespread severe weather \nis not anticipated at this time. Best chances for precip will be \nfrom the Black Hills area through southern parts of SD. Many \nlocations in this area could see half an inch to an inch of \nprecipitation through early Friday morning.\n\nThis shortwave shifts east of the area Friday mid morning with dry\nand mild conditions expected for the daytime period. As the\nwestern upper trof nudges closer to the area, additional chances\nfor light rain will occur across northeastern Wyoming by Friday\nevening, spreading into far western SD after midnight.\n\nUpper trof/closed low crosses the Northern Rockies on Saturday.\nCool and cloudy conditions are expected with rain chances slowly\nincreasing from west to east across the forecast area.\nTemperatures should only rise into the 60s for most areas, with\nnortheastern WY and the Black Hills seeing the best chances for\nprecipitation through Saturday night. A tenth to a quarter inch of\nprecip will be possible. \n\nCoolest and wettest day occurs on Sunday as the upper low tracks\nright across the forecast area. Upper 50s to lower 60s are likely\nfor most locations, with a quarter to half an inch of additional \nprecipitation likely. Upper low shifts slowly east Sunday night,\nwith lingering light precipitation and cool temperatures expected\nto stick around on Monday.\n\nWeak ridging finally makes headway back into the region for the\nmiddle of next week, with milder temperatures moving back into the\narea.",
